<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  tauri-plugin-vpay-checkout's Android host manifest.

  A Tauri v2 Android plugin module is an ordinary `com.android.library`
  subproject of the consuming app's Gradle build, so this file is merged
  into the merchant app's own manifest by AGP's manifest merger. That a
  plugin manifest may declare an `<application>` with `<activity>` elements
  is not an assumption: tauri-apps/plugins-workspace@v2
  `plugins/biometric/android/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml` declares
  `.BiometricActivity` exactly this way, and `plugins/barcode-scanner`'s
  declares `<uses-permission>`/`<uses-feature>` at the manifest root.

  There is no `package=` attribute here: AGP 8 takes the package from
  `android.namespace` in build.gradle.kts ("dev.vpay.tauri.checkout"), which
  is what the leading dot in `.VpayCheckoutActivity` below resolves against.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

  <!--
    `androidx.browser`'s own recommended manifest entry so `CustomTabsIntent`
    can resolve a Custom-Tabs-capable browser under API 30+'s package-
    visibility restrictions (`VpayCheckoutActivity.launchCustomTab`). No
    custom URL scheme entry here or anywhere in this plugin, on purpose —
    ADR-0021/D8.
  -->
  <queries>
    <intent>
      <action android:name="android.support.customtabs.action.CustomTabsService" />
    </intent>
  </queries>

  <application>
    <!--
      D5, revised 2026-09-16 ("browser, not WebView" —
      VpayCheckoutActivity.kt's own header has the full history): a plain
      Activity that launches the payer's browser as a partial (bottom sheet)
      Custom Tab and renders nothing of its own, non-negotiably
      `android:exported="false"`. This Activity takes a URL extra that
      carries a payer credential (the checkout session's own secret, in the
      URL fragment) — an exported Activity accepting that extra is a classic
      intent-redirection hole: any other app on the device could start it
      directly, handing it an attacker-chosen URL to load, or intercepting
      the extra by other means. No other app has any business starting this
      Activity, so it is launched only by this same app's own Tauri plugin
      (`VpayCheckoutPlugin.show`, through Tauri's
      `Plugin.startActivityForResult`, which goes to the app's own
      `ActivityResultLauncher`).

      `Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar` (a stock Android theme — this plugin
      ships no `styles.xml` of its own, and deliberately does not reference
      one of the app's, since a library that assumes the merchant declares
      `@style/AppTheme.Something` fails at merge time in somebody else's
      project) keeps the merchant's own Tauri Activity, one below this one in
      the same task, visible through the brief moments this Activity is on
      screen before the Custom Tab covers it and after it resumes.
    -->
    <activity
      android:name=".VpayCheckoutActivity"
      android:exported="false"
      android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
      android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|keyboardHidden" />

    <!--
      App Link intake. `VpayCheckoutActivity` above stays `exported="false"`
      — see its own comment and file header — and an `exported="false"`
      component cannot be the target of an external Intent, App Links
      included, at all. So this is a SECOND, exported entry point, kept as
      narrow as `VpayCheckoutAppLinkActivity.kt`'s own header can make it:
      it holds no state, never sees the checkout session's credential-
      bearing URL, and only forwards a public success/cancel URL to
      whichever `VpayCheckoutActivity` is currently open. That file's header
      has the full security tradeoff this widening buys and costs — stated
      loudly there and here rather than left implicit.

      **This plugin deliberately declares NO `<intent-filter>` here**, and
      that is the whole point of the entry. `stopUrls` — the merchant's own
      `success_url`/`cancel_url` — are runtime configuration per checkout
      session (D2), so this shared plugin cannot know the host. The filter
      it would otherwise have to write, `<data android:scheme="https"/>`
      with no `android:host`, matches EVERY https URL on the device, and
      that is actively harmful rather than merely useless:

        - `minSdk` here is 21. On API 21-30 an unverified web
          intent-filter still joins the system "Open with" chooser, so
          every merchant app shipping this plugin would offer itself as a
          handler for every https link its payer ever taps, anywhere on
          the device. A payments SDK silently turning its host app into a
          candidate browser is not a tradeoff worth any deep-link
          convenience.
        - On API 31+ Android excludes unverified web links from that
          chooser, so the same filter routes nothing at all. Harmful on
          old Android, inert on new Android — there is no version where a
          hostless filter is the right answer.

      So the merchant declares the filter, for their own verified host, in
      their OWN app's manifest (for a Tauri app that is
      `src-tauri/gen/android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`, which the
      Tauri CLI generates once and then leaves alone), targeting this
      activity by name:

        <activity android:name="dev.vpay.tauri.checkout.VpayCheckoutAppLinkActivity"
                  tools:node="merge">
          <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
            <data android:scheme="https" android:host="shop.example.com" />
          </intent-filter>
        </activity>

      …with `xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"` on that
      manifest's root element, alongside serving a real `assetlinks.json` on
      that host. Until a merchant does both, no deep link reaches this
      activity and every checkout ends as `dismissed` — which D1/D4 already
      make correctness-complete (the poll decides, never the window).

      UNVERIFIED end to end: this repository has no HTTPS domain serving
      `assetlinks.json`, so no deep-link return has ever been driven
      through this activity. It is wired, not proven.
    -->
    <activity
      android:name=".VpayCheckoutAppLinkActivity"
      android:exported="true"
      android:noHistory="true"
      android:excludeFromRecents="true"
      android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar" />
  </application>

</manifest>
